5. REDESIGN THE CREATE NEW SHIPMENT PROCESS

The current shipment creation process should be easier, faster, and more intuitive.

The goal is to let a Desk Admin create a walk-in shipment without navigating multiple confusing pages.

Use a clean step-by-step wizard.

Recommended shipment creation workflow

STEP 1: Customer

* Select existing customer.
* Register new walk-in customer.
* Confirm customer contact details.
* Check KYC status.

STEP 2: Sender and Receiver

Display a clean sender information section.

For walk-in customers:

* Automatically populate sender details from the customer profile.
* Allow authorized staff to edit incorrect details.
* Allow the sender to be different from the customer where applicable.

Receiver information:

* Full name.
* Phone number.
* Email where available.
* Destination country.
* Complete delivery address.
* City and postal code where applicable.

Provide a clear country selector.

STEP 3: Package Details

Allow staff to add one or multiple packages.

For each package, collect:

* Package description.
* Category.
* Quantity.
* Declared value.
* Actual weight where available.
* Package dimensions where operationally required.
* Restricted goods declaration.

Allow staff to:

* Add another package.
* Remove a package.
* Edit package details.
* View the combined shipment summary.

Do not make the user repeatedly re-enter the sender’s information for every package belonging to the same shipment.

STEP 4: SHIPPING SPEED SELECTION

THIS FEATURE IS MANDATORY.

Currently, the shipment creation process does not clearly allow customers to choose priority shipping.

Introduce a dedicated shipping speed selection section before quotation and checkout.

Display two clear options:

STANDARD SHIPPING

* Regular international shipping service.
* Standard estimated delivery window.
* Standard shipping price.
* Suitable for customers who are not in a hurry.

PRIORITY SHIPPING

* Faster available shipping service.
* Priority handling where operationally supported.
* Shorter estimated delivery window where confirmed by the carrier or service provider.
* Additional priority fee.

Show the available options as selectable cards.

Each card should display:

* Service name.
* Estimated delivery window.
* Shipping fee or price difference where available.
* A short explanation.

Make the selected option visually obvious.

Default to Standard Shipping.

Allow the customer or Desk Admin to select Priority Shipping.

IMPORTANT:

Do not invent delivery times or guarantee priority delivery unless the underlying carrier service supports those commitments.

Priority selection must connect to the actual quotation and shipment record.

Save:

* Selected shipping service.
* Priority selection.
* Priority fee.
* Applicable estimated delivery window.
* Service availability.

If Priority Shipping is unavailable for a particular destination or shipment type, disable it and explain why.

The selected service must remain visible throughout checkout and in the shipment receipt.

STEP 5: INSURANCE / CARGO PROTECTION

Allow the customer to choose whether to add available cargo protection.

Show:

* Protection option.
* Declared value.
* Applicable protection fee.
* Brief summary of coverage.
* Link to terms and conditions.

The customer must explicitly select the option.

Do not automatically add protection fees.

Only show coverage and compensation limits that have been approved and configured in the system.

If the protection arrangement is not backed by an authorized insurance or protection provider, do not falsely advertise it as regulated insurance.

Save the selection and associated fee in the shipment record.

STEP 6: INSPECTION AND WEIGHING

The Desk Admin or authorized operational staff should:

* Inspect the package.
* Confirm the declaration.
* Record actual weight.
* Record dimensions if required.
* Upload inspection photos where appropriate.
* Confirm whether the package is accepted.
* Record any exceptions.

Allow the system to update the quotation after the actual weight and required measurements are confirmed.

Maintain an inspection history.

STEP 7: QUOTATION AND CHECKOUT

Create a clean, professional checkout summary.

The customer does not need to understand Swiftvero’s internal operational cost structure.

Do not display confusing internal charge explanations such as:

* Air cargo fuel surcharge.
* Aviation fuel adjustments.
* Internal handling allocation.
* Carrier operating charges.
* Internal logistics calculations.
* Other internal cost components.

These details should remain in authorized internal administrative records if required.

The customer-facing checkout should display ONLY:

SHIPMENT SUMMARY

* Total chargeable weight.
* Selected shipping service: Standard or Priority.
* Insurance / cargo protection fee, if selected.
* Priority fee, if selected.
* Final total amount payable.

Example:

Shipment Weight: 5.0 kg

Shipping Service: Priority

Cargo Protection: ₦3,500

Priority Fee: ₦5,000

TOTAL AMOUNT: ₦XX,XXX

The actual shipping price must come from the existing quotation engine.

The example amounts above are illustrative only.

IMPORTANT:

* Do not invent shipping rates.
* Do not invent insurance prices.
* Do not invent priority fees.
* Use existing approved pricing configurations.
* Ensure all charges are calculated correctly.
* Display only applicable optional fees.
* Do not display an insurance fee when protection is not selected.
* Do not display a priority fee when Standard Shipping is selected.
* Show any legally required tax or mandatory charge clearly, if applicable.
* Keep the final total accurate.

The customer should be able to review the quotation before payment.

Allow the customer to approve or reject the quotation.

STEP 8: PAYMENT AND RECEIPT

Preserve the existing payment architecture.

Support the payment methods already configured.

Show clear payment status:

* Awaiting Payment.
* Payment Processing.
* Paid.
* Failed.
* Refunded.

Do not mark a shipment as paid based only on a frontend success message.

Verify payments through the existing trusted backend/payment gateway process.

After successful payment:

* Generate a shipment tracking number.
* Generate a receipt.
* Display shipment details.
* Send the receipt through available notification channels.
* Update the shipment status.
* Add the shipment to the branch’s operational dashboard.

